fleet.read_log_window has no production callers left after B123 and should be removed or documented as test-only


## Housekeeping, from lane-resolver's B123 work, 2026-09-01

After B123 migrated `probe_enum_corpus` to the raw reader,
`fleet.read_log_window` — the severity-filtered one — **has no production
callers at all.**

lane-resolver deliberately left it in place rather than delete it in the B123
commit, because removing it is a separate mechanical change and it did not want
that riding along in a diff whose argument is about oracle semantics. That was
the right call and this ticket is the consequence.

## Decide, do not drift

Either delete it, or keep it and **document it as test-only in its own
docstring**. What is not acceptable is leaving it looking like a live API: it is
the function whose severity filter caused B118's three false-green channels, and
the next person to reach for the obvious-sounding name will reintroduce them.

If keeping it, say in the docstring WHY the raw reader is the default and what
the filter costs — both build markers are `[ TRAC]`, so `build_slice` returns
zero lines from a filtered window, which is why the build-health check cannot
exist on top of it.

## Check before deleting

Confirm no selftest, mutation harness or doc example depends on it —
`check_probe_cleanup_nonvacuous.py` and `check_enum_probe_nonvacuous.py` both
exercise reader behaviour, and a grep across `scripts/` and `docs/` is the whole
verification. Small ticket; the only way to get it wrong is not to look.


## CORRECTION — this ticket was filed on a wrong premise, 2026-09-01

**"No production callers left" was WRONG when I filed it.** lane-resolver
reported it after B123, its grep having matched a **local variable of the same
name** in `issue_repro_game`. `fleet_dogfood.probe_one` was still calling
`read_log_window` — and that call site held the WORST instance of the very
defect B118 and B127 exist to close. B127 has since fixed that caller.

The lane reported the error against itself. **A claim that a grep found "the
last" of something is worth re-running before it becomes a ticket** — recorded
in the hazards catalogue.

## The ticket still stands, with one caller fewer. But the DECISION has changed.

**Prefer "document as test-only" over "remove".**

`check_enum_probe_nonvacuous.py` deliberately reverts a call site **to**
`read_log_window` as one of its mutations. Deleting the function would break that
mutation's anchor — and it would do so by making the mutation unbuildable rather
than by making it fail, so **a guard would be quietly lost rather than reported
as broken.**

That is the deciding argument and it should be settled before someone deletes it
on the tidy-up reading of the original title.

## Deliverable

1. Confirm by measurement, not grep alone, which callers remain. Run the callers,
   do not just search for the name — that is what produced the wrong premise
   above.
2. Document it in its own docstring as **test-only, retained deliberately as a
   mutation anchor**, naming `check_enum_probe_nonvacuous.py`.
3. Say in the docstring WHY the raw reader is the default and what the filter
   costs: both build markers are `[ TRAC]`, so `build_slice` returns zero lines
   from a filtered window, which is why the build-health check cannot exist on
   top of it.
4. Pin it — a test asserting no production path reaches it, so a future caller
   has to come here and argue rather than drift back.